# FilRunway configuration.
# Copy to .env.local and fill in before switching to live mode.
# .env.local is gitignored; .env.example is the only env file that may be committed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MODE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 'mock' (default) runs MockChainAdapter: no keys, no RPC, no funds. The runway
# drains against accelerated wall-clock time so the whole demo is watchable.
# 'live' runs SynapseChainAdapter against Filecoin Calibration for real.
#
# NOTE: 'live' with a missing or malformed FILECOIN_PRIVATE_KEY fails LOUDLY at
# startup. It deliberately does NOT fall back to the mock -- simulated numbers
# under a "LIVE" badge would be worse than an error page.
FILRUNWAY_MODE=mock

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LIVE MODE CREDENTIALS  (required only when FILRUNWAY_MODE=live)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Private key of the agent wallet on Filecoin Calibration. 32-byte hex, 0x
# prefix optional. Read ONLY by src/lib/chain/synapse.ts and scripts/bootstrap.ts.
# It is never sent to the browser and never logged -- only the derived address is.
#
# TESTNET ONLY. Never put a mainnet key here. Never commit this value.
# The wallet needs tFIL (gas) and USDFC (storage). Calibration faucet:
#   https://faucet.calibnet.chainsafe-fil.io/
FILECOIN_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Calibration RPC endpoint. Optional: unset uses the SDK's built-in
# Calibration fallback set.
FILECOIN_RPC_URL=https://api.calibration.node.glif.io/rpc/v1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEMO TIMESCALE  (optional, default 1 = off)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real Warm Storage pricing is ~$0.029/month for the largest cost stream a demo
# can honestly create (2 copies x 1 GiB + one data set). A 5 USDFC deposit then
# buys roughly 5,200 days of runway, so a 14-day gauge pegs at full and no
# day-based policy threshold ever fires. A judge sees a flat line.
#
# This multiplier scales the agent's POLICY THRESHOLDS and the gauge's
# GRADUATIONS by N. It NEVER touches a value read from the chain: every number
# displayed (funds, burn rate, epochs, days remaining, balances) stays the exact
# onchain reading, and the UI carries a "DEMO TIMESCALE xN - READINGS REAL" badge.
#
# Read it as: "for this demo, treat N days of runway the way a production agent
# would treat 1 day." Behaviour is real; only risk appetite is rescaled.
#
# Use the NEXT_PUBLIC_ form: the gauge is a client component and Next.js only
# inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* into the client bundle. Setting only the server-side
# variable would make the agent act on scaled thresholds while the gauge still
# drew a 14-day axis. `npm run bootstrap -- status` prints a suggested value.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FILRUNWAY_DEMO_SCALE=1

# Server-only fallback, honoured by the policy engine, agent loop and ops CLI
# when the NEXT_PUBLIC_ form is unset. Keep both at the same value if you set it.
FILRUNWAY_DEMO_SCALE=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DECISION JOURNAL  (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An autonomous TOP_UP and `npm run bootstrap -- fund 5` are byte-identical on
# chain. The only evidence that the AGENT authored a transaction is the Decision
# that preceded it, so decisions are appended to an append-only JSONL file that
# survives a server restart. Read it with `npm run decisions`.
#
# LEAVE THIS UNSET. Unset, the path is derived per mode:
#
#   FILRUNWAY_MODE=live  ->  data/decisions.jsonl        (the real record)
#   FILRUNWAY_MODE=mock  ->  data/decisions.mock.jsonl   (simulated)
#
# so a demo run can never append simulated spend into the file that proves the
# agent's real transaction. Both are gitignored.
#
# Setting it explicitly points BOTH modes at that one file. That is still safe —
# every record is stamped MOCK or LIVE and every read is scoped to one mode, so
# the dashboard and `npm run decisions` stay separated either way — but it puts
# the two streams back in one file. Set to `off` to disable persistence and keep
# decisions in memory only, as they were before.
#
# FILRUNWAY_DECISION_LOG=data/decisions.jsonl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEPLOYMENT  (Vercel)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below matters only on a deployed instance. Locally, all of it is
# unset and nothing about `npm run dev` changes.
#
# HOW THE AGENT IS DRIVEN
# The local agent loop is a setInterval. A Vercel Function lives for one
# request, so there is no process to hold a timer: deployed, the cycle is driven
# by something outside the deployment calling /api/tick. Here that is
# .github/workflows/agent-tick.yml, every 5 minutes, with the bearer token
# below; the daily cron job in vercel.ts is only a backstop, because this
# project is on the Hobby plan where cron runs at most once a day.
# The driver is chosen from Vercel's own VERCEL=1 marker; override only if you
# self-host a long-running server on a platform that also sets it.
# FILRUNWAY_AGENT_DRIVER=interval

# There is no cron-schedule variable. The schedule in vercel.ts is a hard-coded
# literal ("0 3 * * *") and has to be: on a git-source deployment the platform
# reads that config STATICALLY, before any build runs, so a process.env
# expression cannot be resolved — the key is dropped and schema validation
# fails with `crons[0]` missing required property `schedule`. Change the
# backstop cadence by editing vercel.ts, not by setting a variable. Change the
# REAL cadence by editing the cron in .github/workflows/agent-tick.yml.

# What the dashboard should BELIEVE the tick cadence is, in ms. Default 60000.
# Read at runtime, and it changes nothing about when a tick happens — only the
# NEXT TICK countdown the dashboard draws.
#
# Set it to match whatever external scheduler is really driving the cycle: a
# scheduler calling /api/tick every 5 minutes with the countdown left at 60s
# would show a clock that expires and then sits there lying. With the shipped
# GitHub Actions workflow (*/5), that is 300000.
# FILRUNWAY_CRON_INTERVAL_MS=300000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TICK AUTHENTICATION  (required on any public deployment)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /api/tick is the only endpoint that can SPEND. On a public URL with a funded
# key in the environment, an unauthenticated version of it lets anyone drain the
# wallet. It therefore requires this shared secret, compared in constant time.
#
# CRON_SECRET is Vercel's own name for it: set it on the project and Vercel Cron
# sends `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET` on every scheduled invocation, so
# the schedule authenticates itself. An operator ticks by hand with the same
# header. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# A deployment with no CRON_SECRET REFUSES every tick (503) rather than falling
# open. Set it before the first deploy. Never commit the value.
# CRON_SECRET=

# Force the check on or off regardless of the driver. Only for tests.
# FILRUNWAY_REQUIRE_TICK_AUTH=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DURABLE JOURNAL ON VERCEL  (Vercel Blob)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A Function's filesystem is read-only apart from an ephemeral /tmp, so
# data/decisions.jsonl cannot survive there — and that file is the only evidence
# that the AGENT, rather than the operator's CLI, authored a transaction. On
# Vercel the journal is written to Vercel Blob instead, as append-only JSONL
# segments, with identical MOCK/LIVE stamping and mode-scoped reads.
#
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN is injected automatically once a Blob store is connected
# to the project; you do not set it by hand. Pull it into .env.local with
# `vercel env pull .env.local` to read the deployed journal locally:
#
#   npm run decisions -- --remote
#
# Locally this is ignored even if present: the local record stays on disk, where
# every existing reference to data/decisions.jsonl expects it.
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=

# Where in the Blob store the journal lives. Default "filrunway/journal".
# FILRUNWAY_BLOB_PREFIX=filrunway/journal

# The store's access mode: "public" or "private". LEAVE THIS UNSET.
#
# A Blob store is provisioned public or private, and @vercel/blob requires an
# explicit access on every write — a public write to a private store is refused
# outright, and a private object cannot be read with a plain fetch at all. This
# once shipped hardcoded to "public" against a private store, which refused
# every append, disabled the journal, and left the dashboard still reporting a
# blob: path to a store holding zero objects.
#
# Unset, the journal resolves the mode itself: observed from the URLs a list()
# returns, and otherwise corrected by retrying a refused write once with the
# other mode. Set this only to skip that one refused request, and know that an
# unrecognised value falls back to detection rather than to a guess.
# FILRUNWAY_BLOB_ACCESS=private

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPENDING SAFETY CAP
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An unattended agent with a funded wallet needs a limit it cannot argue with.
# Over a rolling 24h window the agent will make at most this many deposits, and
# deposit at most this much in total. Reaching either limit produces a SAFETY_CAP
# decision — recorded, with reasoning, in the journal — and NO transaction.
#
# The defaults suit the shipped rule set exactly: one emergency top-up (15 USDFC)
# plus one scheduled top-up (5 USDFC) is 20 USDFC, so the agent can answer a real
# emergency in full and must then stop for the day.
#
# Enforced in LIVE mode only, where real funds move. A MOCK run is capped by
# nothing, so the local demo is unchanged.
# FILRUNWAY_MAX_DEPOSITS_24H=3
# FILRUNWAY_MAX_DEPOSIT_USDFC_24H=20
# FILRUNWAY_SPEND_WINDOW_MS=86400000
# FILRUNWAY_SPEND_CAP=on

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATA SET EVICTION  (destructive, OFF by default)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The agent reads PDP proof state for every data set it pays for: whether the
# set is live (PDPVerifier.dataSetLive), when it last proved
# (getDataSetLastProvenEpoch), when it is next challenged
# (getNextChallengeEpoch), and whether it has proved inside the current period
# (WarmStorage provenThisPeriod / provingDeadline).
#
# A data set that is live, past its proving deadline and unproven is not
# earning its cost. When the runway is ALSO short, the agent decides to
# terminate that data set's payment rail rather than buy runway to keep paying
# for it -- a PRUNE_DATASET decision, recorded in the journal like any other.
#
# Terminating a data set is IRREVERSIBLE: the payment rail winds down and the
# provider stops being paid to keep the pieces. So execution requires this
# explicit opt-in, and it is off unless the value is exactly on / 1 / true / yes.
#
# WITH IT OFF (the default, and what you want for a demo) the agent still MAKES
# and RECORDS the decision, with its target, its reading and its full reasoning
# -- the outcome simply says execution is disabled and names this variable. No
# transaction is attempted and no data is touched.
#
# An UNREADABLE proof state is never delinquency. A reverted or timed-out read
# leaves the state UNKNOWN, the decision says so, and nothing is proposed for
# termination on the strength of it.
# FILRUNWAY_ENABLE_EVICTION=on

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPERATOR SQUEEZE  (the forced-decision control)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The live account has ~2,970 days of runway burning about a day per day, so no
# policy threshold ever fires inside a demo and a judge sees HOLD forever.
#
# POST /api/squeeze withdraws USDFC from Filecoin Pay back to the agent's own
# wallet. That genuinely collapses runwayInEpochs -- the gauge stays a true
# chain reading, it just becomes a small one -- so the agent's next tick has a
# real crisis to answer. Nothing is spent, nothing is simulated, and no funds
# leave the operator's control.
#
# IT IS A HUMAN ACTION. It creates no Decision, adds nothing to the deposits
# tile, and pins a disclosure on the dashboard saying an operator withdrew
# funds. The agent's response is the autonomous part.
#
# It moves money, so it is behind the SAME CRON_SECRET as /api/tick, checked by
# the same constant-time comparison -- but demanded on a STRICTER rule. In LIVE
# mode the secret is required always, on every host and under every driver,
# localhost included: `next dev` with FILRUNWAY_MODE=live is a funded wallet on
# a listening port, and this is the one endpoint that takes funds OUT. In mock
# mode it follows the tick's rule instead (open locally, required on a
# deployment) so the demo and the tests stay frictionless. LIVE with no
# CRON_SECRET set refuses every squeeze with a 503 rather than falling open.
#
# The dashboard's operator controls are inert until a human pastes that secret
# into the page; it is never inlined into the browser bundle, never rendered
# into the HTML and never stored.
#
# Withdrawn when the caller names no amount. Set it to whatever actually
# collapses YOUR account's runway -- `npm run bootstrap -- status` prints the
# balance to size it from.
# FILRUNWAY_SQUEEZE_USDFC=1

# Hard ceiling on a single squeeze, whatever the caller asks for. A request
# above the account's unlocked balance is refused outright rather than clamped.
# FILRUNWAY_MAX_SQUEEZE_USDFC=5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPERATOR WITHDRAWAL CAP  (the rolling budget behind the squeeze)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication answers "who may call /api/squeeze". It does not answer "how
# often", and the two stop being the same question the moment CRON_SECRET is
# published in the README so judges can drive the live demo themselves.
#
# Nothing can be STOLEN through that endpoint -- a withdrawal moves USDFC from
# Filecoin Pay to the agent's own wallet, both ends the same account. But a
# caller looping it would walk the balance to nothing, exhaust the agent's own
# daily deposit allowance answering, and leave a public dashboard showing a
# true, permanent reading of a dead agent.
#
# So withdrawals get their own rolling cap, mirroring the spending cap above and
# counted the same way -- from the DURABLE journal, so it survives the Function
# instance churn a serverless deployment guarantees. Reaching it answers 429
# with a message naming the limit, what has been used and the UTC time it
# relaxes; the dashboard's operator strip shows that line verbatim. No
# transaction is attempted and nothing about what the agent decides changes.
#
# WHERE THE DEFAULTS COME FROM. On the deployed account a healthy runway is
# ~3,966 days against a 3,360-day top-up threshold, and it takes about 2 USDFC
# of withdrawal to cross that -- one crisis-and-recovery cycle. So:
#
#   8 USDFC / 24h  = three full cycles (6 USDFC) plus headroom for one mis-sized
#                    attempt, and no fourth cycle.
#   6 squeezes     = the same three cycles at the 1 USDFC default amount, where
#                    a cycle takes two presses.
#
# Both are anchored to FILRUNWAY_MAX_DEPOSITS_24H=3: the agent can only
# demonstrate recovery three times a day anyway, so a wider withdrawal budget
# would buy nothing but a stretch of unanswerable crisis. And 8 USDFC out is
# deliberately less than the 3 x 5 = 15 USDFC the agent may put back over the
# same window, so a fully exercised demo day cannot leave the account lower than
# it started.
#
# Enforced in LIVE mode only. The mock adapter has no withdraw() at all.
# FILRUNWAY_MAX_SQUEEZES_24H=6
# FILRUNWAY_MAX_SQUEEZE_USDFC_24H=8
# FILRUNWAY_SQUEEZE_WINDOW_MS=86400000
# FILRUNWAY_SQUEEZE_CAP=on

# The floor, as opposed to the rate cap: USDFC that must remain UNLOCKED in
# Filecoin Pay after any withdrawal. A rate cap alone still permits three
# well-sized squeezes that between them empty the account.
#
# LOCKED funds need no setting here -- every request is already bounded against
# Filecoin Pay's own availableFunds, which excludes lockup, so a withdrawal can
# never eat into commitments whatever this is set to.
#
# A runway-THRESHOLD floor ("refuse anything that would cross the emergency
# threshold") was considered and rejected: the emergency rule is a real
# capability, and forbidding the runway from reaching it would make the one
# policy branch nobody could ever demonstrate the one that matters most. 1 USDFC
# is ~125 days at the x480 demo timescale -- well inside the 960-day emergency
# threshold, and safely above zero.
# FILRUNWAY_SQUEEZE_RESERVE_USDFC=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MOCK PROOF STORY  (mock mode only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which PDP proof state the mock adapter reports for its SECOND data set, so the
# eviction paths can be rehearsed with nothing at stake:
#
#   healthy      (default) everything proving on schedule. The agent tops up and
#                never proposes a cut. The local demo, unchanged.
#   delinquent   data set 30292 is live, past its deadline and unproven. A short
#                runway then produces a real PRUNE_DATASET decision.
#   unreadable   its proof calls do not answer. The agent must treat that as
#                UNKNOWN and must NOT propose a cut -- the RPC-hiccup rehearsal.
#
# The first data set stays healthy in every mode, so the account is always mixed
# and the policy engine has to choose rather than blanket-apply.
# FILRUNWAY_MOCK_PROOF=healthy
